+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,165 @@
+# Verified Hilbert–90 problem generator for arXiv:2206.00958
+
+Status: **locally verified, parked pending the required oracle run**. The module
+passes every executable G1–G9 gate, but OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit
+exceeded” for the bare and both diagnostic runs. Consequently there is no STEP 4
+hardness verdict, and this result must not be submitted as hardened yet.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| `TRACK` | B — no-tool compression |
+| native domain | algebra |
+| object regime | finite field |
+| computational core | linear algebra |
+| certificate form | exact symbolic (one fixed-width hexadecimal field element) |
+| intuition | change of variables: remove a repeated rank-one discrepancy, then follow the Frobenius cycle |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none |
+
+## The problem
+
+The source is Faruk Göloğlu’s [*Classification of \((q,q)\)-biprojective APN
+functions*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.00958). Section 2 defines
+GF(2)-linearized polynomials over \(L=\mathrm{GF}(2^n)\), states finite-field
+Hilbert 90, and reduces the relevant kernels and translates to
+\(x^q-x\). Those equations are the exact derivative systems used later to
+classify biprojective APN maps.
+
+An instance asks for the normalized solution of \(x^q+x=c\) in a normal
+basis, after an unknown invertible output-coordinate mixing. It supplies every
+binary equation exactly. The answer is one field element; `verify` checks its
+width and normalization and recomputes all parities. It neither reads the
+planted answer nor invokes the paper. Generation samples the witness first,
+evaluates \(x^q+x\), and applies \(M=I+uv^T\) with \(v^Tu=0\), so the
+certificate is known by construction.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+This is not a Track A claim. Section 2 explicitly explains the reduction to
+\(x^q-x\), and a generic coordinate solver is exact Gaussian elimination in
+\(O(n^3)\) bit operations. At the shipping candidate `easy` (`n=59`), the
+eight-seed reference run solved 8/8 with 38,681 counted operations and roughly
+0.0004 seconds mean wall-clock time. A machine therefore solves these instances
+immediately.
+
+The no-tool gap is structural: subtracting the normal-basis Frobenius-cycle
+rows reveals one repeated discrepancy mask. That identifies the rank-one
+output transvection; after unmixing, one cyclic XOR recurrence gives the
+normalized root. The audited upper bound is 240 word/bit operations. This is
+the paper’s Hilbert–90 mechanism, not an APN yes/no lookup. The complete
+classification in Theorem 1.1 is precisely why the initially proposed task
+“decide whether this listed construction is APN” was not used.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=7, k=2, seed=0)`, the complete rendered data are:
+
+```text
+Solve an exact output-mixed Hilbert--90 equation over GF(2^7).
+Use the normal basis beta_e=theta^(2^e), e=0,...,6.  Raising to q=2^2
+moves coordinates by e -> e+2 (mod 7).  Each row is "label: rhs | mask";
+bit e of the two-digit hexadecimal mask is the coefficient of x_e.
+
+  r0: 0 | 28
+  r6: 0 | 59
+  r1: 1 | 42
+  r2: 1 | 05
+  r5: 1 | 21
+  r3: 1 | 0a
+  r4: 0 | 1d
+
+Find the unique solution with x_0=0 and return exactly two lowercase hex digits.
+<answer>00</answer> is only a format example.
+```
+
+The planted answer is `<answer>6c</answer>`.
+
+```python
+verify(inst, "6c")  # (True, "ok")
+verify(inst, "4c")  # (False, "equation r0 has parity 1, expected 0")
+```
+
+This seven-coordinate demo is genuinely hand-solvable by either elimination or
+the rank-one recurrence; it is the smallest supported setting.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | n | k | normalized space | answer width | seed-0 Gaussian cost | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 7 | 2 | \(2^6\) | 2 hex digits | 276 operations | hand example; never ships |
+| easy | 59 | 13 | \(2^{58}\) | 15 hex digits | 37,516 operations | shipping candidate; oracle unavailable |
+| medium | 67 | 17 | \(2^{66}\) | 17 hex digits | 52,061 operations | not reached |
+| hard | 71 | 23 | \(2^{70}\) | 18 hex digits | 54,071 operations | not reached |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` currently names `easy`, provisionally. It may only become
+the actual shipping rung after a successful bare `harden.py` verdict.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 planted witnesses verified; answers JSON-round-trip |
+| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged uppercase answer amid prose/fences parsed and verified |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware uniform guesses; space \(2^{58}\) |
+| G5 | pass | exact shipping density \(2^{-58}\); demo has exactly one answer; reference cost recorded |
+| G6 | pass | six attacks × eight seeds, 0 successes; Gaussian reference 8/8; compact route 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=118` instance built and verified; answer-space exponent grew 58→117 |
+| G8 | pass | 140/140 relabellings invariant and real; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 17 JSON characters, one field element (59 binary coordinates), 5 estimated tokens, 240 intended operations |
+
+The six failing attacks were dense-row majority voting, interpreting the RHS
+as coordinates, display-order greedy assignment, ignoring the output mixing,
+using the discrepancy indicator as the answer, and 256 uniform normalized
+restarts. Gaussian elimination is correctly reported as Track B’s successful
+reference algorithm, not hidden in `attacks`.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The script-owned bare transcript contains four service errors and no scored
+attempt. Since the service failed before a reply, none of these rows is evidence
+that a model failed the problem.
+
+| arm/model | calls | scored attempts | solved | outcome |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| bare / OpenAI Terra | 4 | 0 | 0 | HTTP 403 key limit; no verdict |
+| structural / Gemini Flash | 4 | 0 | 0 | HTTP 403 key limit; no verdict |
+| placebo / Terra + Gemini | 4 | 0 | 0 | HTTP 403 key limit; no verdict |
+
+Hinted minus placebo is unavailable, not zero: both denominators are zero. No
+conclusion about the claimed `change of variables` intuition is possible until
+the arms are rerun. The answer-size and intended-route figures above are valid
+local measurements and remain gated independently.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2206_00958 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=123, **DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+print(render(inst))
+ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+assert (ok, reason) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After OpenRouter access is restored, rerun the bare loop here and the structural
+and placebo loops in separate scratch directories. Once it reports
+`hardened`, emit from the repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2206.00958 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The family exercises the Hilbert–90 engine used by the paper, not the full APN
+classification theorem. It becomes easy as soon as the repeated rank-one row
+discrepancy is recognized; that is intentional for Track B. The random-guess
+density is uniform over all fixed-width normalized field elements and says
+nothing about a solver with structural priors. Gaussian elimination makes the
+family trivial with tools. I did not test Wiedemann-style sparse linear algebra,
+SAT/SMT encodings, or a learned rank-one detector; those can only strengthen the
+statement that tools solve it. Most importantly, the mandatory multi-vendor
+no-tool test did not run because the external key was exhausted. Until it does,
+the local attack panel supports correctness and construction hygiene but not the
+required model-hardness claim.
