   106	
   107	\title{Classification of $(q,q)$-biprojective APN functions}%
   108	\author{Faruk G\"{o}lo\u{g}lu}
   109	\address{MFF, Charles University, Prague.}
   110	\curraddr{}
   111	\email{faruk.gologlu@mff.cuni.cz}
   112	\thanks{MFF, Charles University, Prague. ({\tt faruk.gologlu@mff.cuni.cz})\\
   113	This work was supported by the {\sf GA\v{C}R Grant 18-19087S - 301-13/201843}.}
   114	
   115	
   116	\date{}
   117	
   118	\dedicatory{}
   119	
   120	\begin{abstract}
   121	In this paper, we classify $(q,q)$-biprojective almost perfect nonlinear (APN) 
   122	functions over $\LL \times \LL$ under the natural left and right action of 
   123	$\GL(2,\LL)$ where $\LL$ is a finite field of characteristic $2$. 
   124	This shows in particular that the only quadratic APN functions (up to $\CCZ$-equivalence)
   125	over $\LL \times \LL$ that satisfy the so-called subfield property 
   126	are the Gold functions and the function $\kappa : \f{64} \to \f{64}$ 
   127	which is the only known APN function that is equivalent to a permutation over 
   128	$\LL \times \LL$ up to $\CCZ$-equivalence. The $\kappa$-function was introduced in
   129	(Browning, Dillon, McQuistan, and Wolfe, 2010). Deciding whether there exist other 
   130	quadratic APN functions (possibly $\CCZ$-equivalent to permutations) that satisfy
   131	subfield property or equivalently, generalizing $\kappa$ to higher dimensions was an
   132	open problem listed for instance in (Carlet, 2015) as one of the interesting open 
   133	problems on cryptographic functions.
   134	\end{abstract}
   135	
   136	\maketitle
   137	
   138	\section{Introduction}
   139	
   140	Almost perfect nonlinear (APN) functions are cryptographically important
   141	functions over a vector space over the finite field of order two that provides 
   142	the best resistance against differential cryptanalysis.
   143	Arguably the most important open problem on APN functions is the
   144	question on the existence of APN permutations over even dimensional vector spaces.
   145	There are no APN permutations over $\f{2}^2$ and $\f{2}^4$ \cite{apnfour}. 
   146	Existence of an APN permutation over $\f{2}^6$ was shown in \cite{jfd10}.
   147	The function is $\CCZ$-equivalent (see Sections \ref{sec_2} and \ref{sec_3} 
   148	for the definitions of the concepts that are used in Introduction) to
   149	the quadratic function $\kappa : \f{2^6} \to \f{2^6}$. Note that we will view the
   150	finite field $\f{2^{2l}}$ as a vector space $\f{2}^{2l}$ and also as 
   151	$\f{2^l}\times \f{2^l}$. When $\kappa$ is viewed as a polynomial over the 
   152	finite field $\f{2^6}$ it falls into the class of Dembowski-Ostrom polynomials
   153	that satisfy the subfield property introduced in \cite{jfd10}. These 
   154	polynomial functions when viewed as functions over $\f{2^l} \times \f{2^l}$
   155	form the class of $(q,q)$-biprojective functions. Of course a natural 
   156	question that arises is to determine whether Dembowski-Ostrom polynomials
   157	that satisfy the subfield property (or, equivalently, the class of $(q,q)$-biprojective 
   158	functions) contain APN functions that are $\CCZ$-equivalent to permutations for $l > 3$.
   159	The question is viewed as an important problem. For instance it was included 
   160	by Carlet in a list of interesting research questions regarding crytographic functions
   161	\cite[Section 3.7]{Carlet15} as an important subproblem of the major problem of deciding 
   162	whether there exists an APN permutation when $l > 3$. 
   163	The problem also appeared in \cite[Problem 15]{G15}. 
   164	In this paper, we solve this problem. 
   165	
   166	\begin{theorem}\label{thm_main}
   167	Let $q = 2^k$, $r = 2^l$, $\LL = \f{2^l}$ with $0 < k < l$ and 
   168	$F : \LL \times \LL \to \LL \times \LL$
   169	be a $(q,q)$-biprojective function. Then $F$ is APN if and only if $\Gcd(k,l) = 1$, and
   170	\begin{enumerate}
   171	\item $l$ is even and $F \GLEQ G_{q+1}$ or $F \GLEQ G_{q+r}$, or
   172	\item $l$ is odd, $k$ is odd, and  $F \GLEQ G_{q+1}$, or 
   173	\item $l$ is odd, $k$ is even, and $F \GLEQ G_{q+r}$, or
   174	\item $l = 3$ and $F \GLEQ \kappa$.
   175	\end{enumerate}
   176	\end{theorem}
   177	
   178	We have the following clarifications for the statement of the theorem.
   179	\begin{itemize}
   180	
   181	
   182	\item  The equivalence relation $\GLEQ$ is introduced by 
   183	the action of $\GL(2,\LL) \times \GL(2,\LL)$ which is a finer notion of 
   184	equivalence than that of $\CCZ$-equivalence. Thus our results imply 
   185	$\CCZ$-equivalence results.
   186	
   187	\item  The biprojective maps $G_s : \LL \times \LL \to \LL \times \LL$ 
   188	with $s \in \{q+1,q+r\}$ are 
   189	the so-called Gold maps $X \mapsto X^s$ in the univariate notation
   190	with a suitable identification of the vector spaces. 
   191	
   192	\item The Gold maps $G_{2^i(2^j+1)}$ over $\LL \times \LL$ are APN whenever 
   193	$\Gcd(j,2l) = 1$ by \cite{gold}. APN Gold maps over $\LL \times \LL$ are not 
   194	$\CCZ$-equivalent to permutations by \cite{GL20}. 
   195	Thus, a $(q,q)$-biprojective APN function $F$ over $\LL \times \LL$ is 
   196	$\CCZ$-equivalent to a permutation if and only if $l = 3$ and $F \GLEQ \kappa$.
   197	
   198	\item The special case $k = 1$ was solved in \cite{CL21} using
   199	results from \cite{LLHQ,Dasa,GKL}. Thus, our theorem generalizes the main results
   200	of \cite{CL21,LLHQ,Dasa,GKL}.
   201	
   202	\item Another idea to attack the problem is to identify a class of functions
   203	that includes $\kappa$ when $l = 3$ and also are $\CCZ$-equivalent 
   204	to permutations for larger $l$. This is the case of the so-called butterfly 
   205	construction \cite{PUB} which requires $l$ to be odd. In \cite{CPT,CDP}, butterflies
   206	were shown not to be APN when $l > 3$. We will show in Remark \ref{rem_butterfly} that 
   207	a subcase of our Proposition \ref{prop_s} (which is a subcase of the main theorem) 
   208	strictly generalizes the butterfly construction. Thus, our theorem 
   209	also generalizes the main results of \cite{CPT,CDP}.
   210	
   211	\item The proof is based on three concepts:
   212	\begin{itemize}
   213	\item zeroes of projective polynomials \cite{Bluher},
   214	\item properties of Dillon-Dobbertin difference sets \cite{DD}, and
   215	\item recent classification of fractional projective permutations over finite fields \cite{FG22FFA}.
   216	\end{itemize}
   217	
   218	\item The proof avoids the use of Weil bound and is purely combinatorial.
   219	\end{itemize}
   220	
   221	The natural actions of the groups
   222	$\GL(2,\LL) \times \GL(2,\LL)$ 
   223	(left and right application of non-singular $\LL$-linear transformations)
   224	and
   225	($\LLx \times \LLx) \times \GL(2,\LL)$ 
   226	(scaling on both components and right application of non-singular $\LL$-linear transformations) 
   227	on bivariate vectorial Boolean functions of type
   228	\[
   229	F : \LL \times \LL \to \LL \times \LL
   230	\]
   231	and the action of $\LLx \times \GL(2,\LL)$ on bivariate vectorial Boolean functions of type
   232	\[
   233	f : \LL \times \LL \to \LL 
   234	\]
   235	are important for this paper. The set of $(q,q')$-biprojective functions 
   236	is fixed (setwise) by the action $(\LLx \times \LLx) \times \GL(2,\LL)$ 
   237	(actually, $(q,q')$-biprojectivity is defined in such a way to accommodate this property). 
   238	This and other niceties introduced by $\PGL(2,\LL)$ on $q$-projective functions
   239	allows one to prove the rather straightforward fact \cite[Lemma 3.6]{FG22IEEE} that  
   240	whether these functions are APN can be checked in a simple way using 
   241	parametrization from $\ProjectiveLine(\LL)$ instead of $\LL \times \LL$
   242	which also hints why we have many such families. 
   243	A method to find new biprojective APN families
   244	was given as well in  \cite[Lemma 4.2]{FG22IEEE} along with two biprojective APN families. 
   245	These group actions and biprojectivity were instrumental in giving a method 
   246	(in joint works with Lukas K\"olsch) to check 
   247	equivalences between biprojective APN functions \cite[Theorem 3]{GK21} and 
   248	isotopisms between biprojective semifields \cite[Theorem 5.10]{GK22}, which 
   249	allowed us to solve the equivalence problem for all known biprojective APN 
   250	families and, in the semifields case, generalizing a result of Albert 
   251	\cite{Albert} to give a solution to a 60-year old problem of Hughes 
   252	\cite{Hughes} on determining the autotopism group of Knuth semifields 
   253	\cite{GKPP}. 
   254	The first ever family of commutative semifields of 
   255	odd order that contains an exponential number of non-isotopic semifields 
   256	\cite[Corollary 6.4]{GK22}, and a family containing an exponential number of inequivalent
   257	APN functions \cite[Theorem 5]{GK21} were given recently using this method. 
   258	In the case of APN functions, Kaspers and Zhou 
   259	were the first \cite{KZ} to prove such a result with a different method. 
   260	They showed that the Taniguchi family \cite{Taniguchi}, which is also 
   261	$(q,q')$-biprojective, contains an exponential number of inequivalent APN 
   262	functions. In the $(q,q)$-biprojective 
   263	case, even the larger action $\GL(2,\LL) \times \GL(2,\LL)$ fixes the set of 
   264	$(q,q)$-biprojective functions (setwise). This fact can be seen as the main reason that
   265	the classification in this paper is possible which we will heavily exploit
   266	(see Section \ref{sec_3} ff.) together with the above mentioned properties introduced
   267	by these classical groups on $(q,q)$-biprojective functions.
   268	Note that these are natural and well-known group actions which were used
   269	previously on biprojective semifields \cite{bbeven,bbodd}.
   270	
   271	
   272	Of course, the study of bivariate functions and semifields (not necessarily 
   273	biprojective) has a long history. In the case of semifields, the bivariate idea 
   274	dates back almost a century to Dickson \cite{Dickson}, Hughes and Kleinfeld 
   275	\cite{HK} and Knuth \cite{Knuth}.
   276	For the APN functions, the initial work on bivariate functions was by Carlet 
   277	\cite{CarletBivariate} who found the first biprojective APN family, and then 
   278	Zhou and Pott \cite{ZP} introduced another family of biprojective APN functions
   279	as well as a family of commutative semifields that contains a quadratic number of 
   280	inequivalent members. Carlet, then introduced \cite{CarletSCM} a method to find 
   281	bivariate (but not necessarily biprojective) APN functions from his previous 
   282	biprojective family. Further work on biprojective APN functions includes the 
   283	family of Taniguchi \cite{Taniguchi}, and quite recently the papers 
   284	\cite{LiZhou,CLV} 
   285	which derive both biprojective and non-biprojective APN families from the 
   286	family of \cite{FG22IEEE} (or by extending it). The method of \cite{CarletSCM} 
   287	was further investigated in \cite{CBC}. Further work that do not involve 
   288	constructions of bivariate functions but study their important 
   289	properties include \cite{Anbar,KZ2,KKK,Kaspers}.
   290	
   291	In Section \ref{sec_2} we will explain the notions related to vectorial 
   292	Boolean functions, including the definitions of biprojective functions,
   293	Dembowski-Ostrom polynomials and APN functions that we mentioned in Introduction. 
   294	In Section \ref{sec_3} we will explain the various actions we introduced 
   295	above (and more) on vectorial functions and the corresponding equivalence 
   296	relations including $\approx_{\CCZ}$ and $\GLEQ$ mentioned above. We also 
   297	determine the equivalence classes of one of these actions which will give 
   298	us a \textit{representative set} of biprojective functions which will 
   299	reduce the problem of classification of all biprojective functions to 
   300	the problem of classification of functions in the representative set.
   301	Section \ref{sec_4} contains results required in the proof of the main 
   302	theorem related to zeroes of projective polynomials, Dillon-Dobbertin 
   303	difference sets and the recent classification of fractional projective 
   304	permutations over finite fields. Finally, in Section \ref{sec_5}, we prove 
   305	our main theorem.
