   314	\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec_2}
   315	
   316	Let $F : \f{2}^n \to \f{2}^n$ be a \textbf{vectorial Boolean function}.
   317	A function $F$ is said to be \textbf{almost perfect nonlinear (APN)}
   318	if 
   319	\[
   320		F(x) + F(x+a) = b
   321	\]
   322	has zero or two solutions for every
   323	$(a,b) \in \f{2}^n \setminus \{0\} \times \f{2}^n$. Every vectorial
   324	Boolean function $F : \f{2}^n \to \f{2}^n$ can be written as 
   325	an evaluation function of a polynomial over $\f{2^n}[X]$ with 
   326	(polynomial) degree at most $2^n-1$, i.e., $F : X \mapsto F(X)$ where
   327	\[
   328	 F(X) = \sum_{i = 0}^{2^n-1} A_i X^i.
   329	\]
   330	We will not make distinction between functions and their polynomial
   331	representations. The \textbf{algebraic degree} of a vectorial Boolean
   332	function is defined to be
   333	\[
   334	\algdeg(F) = \max \{ \wt_2(i) \ : \ A_i \ne 0 \},
   335	\]
   336	where $\wt_2$ denotes the weight of base-$2$ representation of an integer.
   337	When we say \textit{quadratic}, \textit{affine} or \textit{linear}, we refer to this
   338	notion of degree. We will need the usual \textbf{polynomial degree}
   339	as well which will be denoted by 
   340	\[
   341	\deg(F) = \max \{ i \ : \ A_i \ne 0 \}
   342	\]
   343	as usual. 
   344	The polynomials in $\f{2^n}[X]$ that correspond to \textbf{affine} functions are
   345	\[
   346	M(X) = \sum_{0 \le i \le n-1} C_i X^{2^i} + D.
   347	\]
   348	If $D = 0$, they are called \textbf{linear} functions and are $\f{2}$-linear 
   349	vector-space endomorphisms of $\f{2^n}$ when viewed as a $\f{2}$-vector space.
   350	As polynomials in $\f{2^n}[X]$, they are known as \textbf{linearized polynomials}.
   351	We are particularly interested in the class of quadratic polynomials in $\f{2^n}[X]$,
   352	\[
   353	 Q(X) = \sum_{0 \le i \ne j \le n-1} B_{ij} X^{2^i+2^j} +
   354	       \sum_{0 \le i \le n-1} C_i X^{2^i} +
   355				 D.
   356	\]
   357	The subclass of the above class of polynomials that contain only the 
   358	non-affine parts is known as \textbf{Dembowski-Ostrom (DO)} polynomials, i.e.,
   359	\[
   360	D(X) = \sum_{0 \le i \ne j \le n-1} B_{ij} X^{2^i+2^j}.

exec
