   462	\subsection{The trace map and Hilbert's Theorem 90}
   463	Let $\LL$ be the finite field with $p^l$ elements, $q = p^k$ for $k > 0$
   464	and let $\DD \subset \LL$ of order $p^\delta$ with $\delta = \Gcd(k,l)$. 
   465	The \textbf{trace map} is defined as
   466	\[
   467	\tr{\LL/\DD}{x} = \sum_{j = 0}^{l/\delta-1} x^{(p^\delta)^j}.
   468	\]
   469	When $\DD = \f{p}$ then we simply write
   470	\[
   471	\trace{x} = \tr{\LL/\f{p}}{x}. 
   472	\]
   473	
   474	The following is the finite fields version of Hilbert's Theorem 90.
   475	
   476	\begin{lemma}[Hilbert's Theorem 90]\label{lem_hilbert}
   477	Let $\Gcd(j,l) = 1$ and $a \in \LL$. Then 
   478	$\tr{\LL/\DD}{a} = 0$ if and only if $a = x^{(p^\delta)^j} - x$ for some $x \in \LL$.
   479	\end{lemma}
   480	
   481	The $\DD$-linear vector-space endomorphisms of $\LL$ can be written as
   482	\[
   483	L(x) = \sum_{j = 0}^{l/\delta-1} a_j x^{(p^\delta)^j}, \quad a_j \in \LL,
   484	\]
   485	and are called \textbf{$\DD$-linearized polynomials}. Determining kernels of such 
   486	endomorphisms in $\LL$, especially of the form $L(x) = ax^q - bx$ and the 
   487	zeroes of its translates $L(x)+c$, is important for this paper. This can 
   488	simply be done by observing
   489	\[
   490	ax^q - bx = 0
   491	\]
   492	for some nonzero $r \in \LL$ if and only if $r^{q-1} = b/a$. In that case
   493	$L(rx)/rb = x^q - x$.
   494	
   495	Then one can deduce that the zeroes of $L(x)$ are $0$ and $\epsilon r$ for 
   496	$\epsilon \in \DDx$ if such $r$ exists. Then the case $L(x)+c$ can be handled 
   497	using Hilbert's Theorem 90. The following lemma is relevant and will be needed.
   498	
   499	\begin{lemma}\label{lem_gcd}
   500	For a prime $p$,
   501	\begin{enumerate}
   502	\item $\Gcd(p^k-1,p^l-1) = p^{\Gcd(k,l)}-1$.
   503	\item 
   504	\[
   505	\Gcd(p^k+1,p^l-1) = \left\{\begin{array}{ll}
   506	1 & \textrm{if } \frac{l}{\Gcd(k,l)} \textrm{ is odd, and } p = 2,\\
   507	2 & \textrm{if } \frac{l}{\Gcd(k,l)} \textrm{ is odd, and } p \textrm{ is odd},\\
   508	p^{\Gcd(k,l)}+1  & \textrm{if } \frac{l}{\Gcd(k,l)} \textrm{ is even}.
   509	\end{array}\right.
   510	\]
   511	\end{enumerate}
   512	\end{lemma}
   513	
