   516	\subsection{Difference sets with Singer parameters and multisets}
   517	
   518	Let $M$ be a multiset whose elements are $s_i$ with repetition $d_i$ for $1 \le i \le r$, denoted by
   519	\[
   520	M = \lms s_1^{[d_1]}, \ldots, s_r^{[d_r]} \rms,
   521	\]
   522	where we write $\mult_M(s_i) = d_i$. 
   523	When all $s_i$ have the same repetition number $d$, we write $M = S^{[d]}$,
   524	where $S = \{ s_1, \ldots, s_r \}$.
   525	
   526	For two multisets $S,T$ we denote by
   527	\[
   528	S/T = \lms s/t \ : \ s \in S, t \in T \rms
   529	\]
   530	the \textit{direct division} of two multisets.
   531	
   532	A subset $D$ of cardinality $k$ of a group $G$ (written multiplicatively) 
   533	of order $v$ is said to be a \textbf{$(v,k,\lambda)$-difference set} if
   534	\[
   535	D/D = \{1\}^{[k]} \cup (G \setminus \{1\})^{[\lambda]}.
   536	\]
   537	
   538	We are mostly interested in the cyclic difference sets in the multiplicative
   539	group $\LLx$ in characteristic $2$. The parameters 
   540	$(2^l-1,2^{l-1},2^{l-2})$ and
   541	$(2^l-1,2^{l-1}-1,2^{l-2}-1)$ are called \textbf{Singer parameters}, since they
   542	are the parameters of the Singer sets (i.e., hyperplanes of $\LL$),
   543	\[
   544	\Hbar = \{ x \in \LL \ : \ \trace{x} = 1 \},
   545	\]
   546	and $\HH \setminus \{0\}$ where
   547	\[
   548	\HH = \{ x \in \LL \ : \ \trace{x} = 0 \},
   549	\]
   550	respectively. Dillon-Dobbertin difference sets are another important example of
   551	cyclic difference sets with Singer parameters \cite{DD} (see Lemmas \ref{lem_dd}, \ref{lem_multiset}) 
   552	which we will use frequently in this paper.
   553	
   554	\subsection{Equivalences of vectorial Boolean functions}
   555	
   556	Let $F : \FF \to \FF$ be a vectorial Boolean function and $\FF = \f{2^n}$.
   557	The widest known notion of equivalence that keeps the APN property invariant
   558	is called the \textbf{$\CCZ$-equivalence} \cite{CCZ}. Define the \textbf{graph} 
   559	of the function $F$ by
   560	\[
   561	\Gamma_F = \{ (x,F(x)) \ : \ x \in \FF \}.
   562	\]
   563	Then $F$ is said to be $\CCZ$-equivalent to $F'$ if there exists $\f{2}$-linear endomorphisms
   564	$A,B,C,D$ of $\FF$, elements $u,v \in \FF$ and a permutation $\pi : \FF \to \FF$ such that
   565	\begin{equation}\label{eq_CCZ}
   566	\begin{pmatrix}
   567	A & B \\
   568	C & D
   569	\end{pmatrix}
   570	\begin{pmatrix}
   571	x\\
   572	F(x)
   573	\end{pmatrix}
   574	+
   575	\begin{pmatrix}
   576	u\\
   577	v
   578	\end{pmatrix}
   579	=
   580	\begin{pmatrix}
   581	\pi(x)\\
   582	F'(\pi(x))
   583	\end{pmatrix}.
   584	\end{equation}
   585	In that case we write $F \approx_{\CCZ} F'$. A narrower notion of equivalence that keeps
   586	the algebraic degree of $F$ invariant is called the \textbf{extended affine (EA) equivalence}.
   587	We write $F \approx_{\EA} F'$ if 
   588	\[
   589	A_1 \circ F \circ A_2(x) + A_3(x) = F'(x),
   590	\]
   591	for affine maps $A_1,A_2,A_3 : \FF \to \FF$ with $A_1,A_2$ bijective. It can easily be shown 
   592	that $\EA$-equivalence is a special case of $\CCZ$-equivalence where one sets $B = 0$.
   593	An important theorem for quadratic APN functions is that for two quadratic APN functions 
   594	$F,G$ we have by a result of Yoshiara \cite{Yoshiara},
   595	\[
   596	F \approx_{\EA} G \iff F \approx_{\CCZ} G.
   597	\]
   598	We can restrict the equivalence even more if, for instance, we want to keep the property 
   599	of being a permutation invariant. 
   600	The functions $F$ and $F'$ are said to be \textbf{linearly equivalent} if 
   601	\[
   602	L_1 \circ F \circ L_2 = F',
   603	\]
   604	for $L_1,L_2 \in \GL(n,2)$. Note that this is equivalent to setting $B = C = 0$ and 
   605	$u = v = 0$ in \eqref{eq_CCZ} 
   606	(when only $B = C = 0$ holds, they are called \textbf{affinely equivalent}).
   607	We denote this equivalence by $F \approx_{\GL(n,2)} F'$. In the following, we will 
   608	even restrict this equivalence to the case where $L_1,L_2 \in \GL(2,2^l) < \GL(2l,2)$ 
   609	when $n = 2l$ with the obvious motivation that the (left and right) action of 
   610	$\GL(2,2^l)$ keeps the $(q,q)$-biprojective property of $F(x,y) = (f(x,y),g(x,y))$ invariant.
   611	
